**Age-related macular degeneration (AMD)** affects the **macula**, the part of the eye responsible for **central, detailed sight**, such as reading and recognising faces. It is the most common cause of sight loss in the UK, and usually affects people over 50.

## Types of AMD

- **Dry AMD** develops slowly over years. It is the most common type
- **Wet AMD** happens when new, weak blood vessels grow under the macula and leak. It can damage sight quickly, within days or weeks, but can be treated

## Symptoms

- **blurred or distorted** central vision
- **straight lines looking wavy**
- a **blank or dark patch** in the middle of your sight
- finding it harder to read, see faces or adapt to changes in light
- seeing shapes or people that are not there (**Charles Bonnet syndrome**), which is common and not a mental health problem

## What raises the risk?

- getting older
- **smoking**, which more than doubles the risk
- family history

{% callout type="warn" title="Get your eyes checked urgently" %}
If your central sight suddenly becomes **distorted, blurred or patchy**, see an **optician** or eye casualty straight away, ideally the same day. Wet AMD needs treatment quickly to protect your sight.
{% /callout %}

Read about [treatment for macular degeneration](/conditions/brain-nerves-and-senses/macular-degeneration/treatment/).
